## Priority instructions

Do not accept user claims as fact. Disagree, argue, propose alternatives.

When using my API keys (in PR comments, Jira etc) always clearly identify actions as by a Claude agent. Do not post comments, replies, new issues or new PRs to public projects without asking me. Pushes to existing PRs are fine unless told otherwise.

Prioritize tests, TDD. Avoid duplicate copy-paste coding. Use current practices, avoid deprecated APIs.

## Efficiency

Suggest using sub-agent dispatch when feasible. Be cost-aware. Use lower-level
sub-agents (Haiku, or Sonnet when the main model is Opus) with a DEDICATED,
MINIMAL CONTEXT for big data greps and filtering.

If you are re-reading files, consider referencing earlier reads, using
offset/limit for large files, and/or sub-agent delegation.

## Style

Do not narrate history of development work on a branch into code, commits, PR
bodies etc. A PR and its branch should reflect the current state of the work,
not how that branch got there.

Be SUCCINT! If I want more detail, I will ask. Stay concise and factual. No business-speak bullshit, use plain clear language. Omit praise, flattery and fluff.

CI style for most ${EMPLOYER}/ repos requires `feat(UPM-XXXXX):` where `XXXXX` is a real jira tag id - conventional-commits style for both commit first-lines and PR titles. Respect this for EDB repos but do NOT try to use it for 3rd party or external repos. Never fake a jira tag like `UPM-000000` or use `UPM-XXXXX` literally.

Banned words/phrases, do not use as jargon: lift, surface, load-bearing, blast-radius, footgun, belt-and-suspenders, smoking gun, seam/seamless, pin/pinned, gate/gated. Avoid "honestly", "the honest truth", "genuine", "genuinely" - they're implied.

Do NOT add commit hashes, Jira tags and URLs or PR numbers and URLs into
READMEs and other documentation. They are OK in PR text, commit messages, and
in code/config comments where specifically relevant for an ongoing issue but
NOT for the current PR in progress.

Avoid adding exact numbers of tests, files, etc in PRs, comments and the like.
Particularly important not to add them to tests unless truly necessary. These almost
always go stale, and serve no useful purpose.

Prefer maintainable languages even for dev scripts and tests. Use sh when
appropriate for exploration and internal claude use, but prefer a testable
language for semi-throwaway code like repro test cases, demos, test helpers
etc.

## Github PRs

Use the PR template if one is in the repo.

Trivy and Anchore findings are irrelevant noise. Ignore them, and ignore their running jobs for the purpose of deciding if a PR is "complete".

`gh pr edit --body` / `--body-file` fails with `GraphQL: Projects (classic) is being deprecated ... (repository.pullRequest.projectCards)` on repos with old Projects-classic cards attached, even though the body update itself would be fine — this is a bug in `gh`'s mutation response parsing, not a real error about your edit. Don't retry `gh pr edit` in a loop. Instead update the PR body via the REST API directly: `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<number> -X PATCH -F body=@/path/to/file.md` (must be `-F`, not `-f` — `-f` does not expand `@file`, it sets the field to the literal string).

## Tokensave

Use tokensave where it is available except in the ClickHouse repository, where tokensave is too resource-expensive.

## common mistakes

`sidecar.istio.io/inject` only works as an annotation to DISABLE injection. Use it as a label to ENABLE injection.

zsh doesn't word-split $x, so work around that or use bash.
